**Theorem 2.3 (Finite fixed ports for the split generators).** For a
generator on the right of (2.2), the full unnormalized Choi matrix is

$$
Z(k(a(p),b))=\frac1{25}\sum_{k,l=0}^4
(g_{kl}a(p)a(p)^Tg_{kl}^T)\otimes(g_{kl}bb^Tg_{kl}^T),
\quad g_{kl}=G(2\pi k/5,2\pi l/5).
\tag{2.8}
$$

Consequently a TP sum of $m$ such generators has a realization by
at most $25m$ fixed scalar flags. Applying Theorem 2.2 to any
finite generator decomposition therefore supplies an exact finite
realization. The same whole channel also retains the credited
at-most-$225$ real-product realization bound.

Proof. The joint product projector for $a(p),b$ is fixed by
$g_*\otimes g_*$: each of its two projector factors is fixed
separately. As $\mathsf J=G(\pi,\pi)g_*$ and $\mathsf J$ reverses
the torus angles, the joint torus average of this projector is already
$\mathsf J\otimes\mathsf J$ invariant. Its full torus-and-reflection
average is therefore just its torus average. QRC Theorem 5.2 identifies
that average with $Z(k(a(p),b))$.

Every entry of a joint product projector contains four vector
coordinates. Each vector coordinate under $G(\theta,\phi)$ has
frequencies in $\{0,\pm e_1,\pm e_2\}$. The resulting Fourier support
is contained in $|m|+|n|\le4$. The five-point sum in each angle
annihilates a frequency unless both $m,n$ are divisible by five.
Only the zero frequency in this support survives. This proves (2.8)
entry by entry. This is the elementary finite Fourier mechanism
credited to [QFA, Lemma 2.2](RECURSIVE_RELATIONAL_OBSERVATION_QUANTUM_FINITE_PORT_ATTAINMENT.md)
and QRC Theorem 5.2, with the reflection made redundant by (2.4).

For a TP sum, assign to each of these finitely many projectors its
nonnegative coefficient $c_f$, with unit real preparation and
measurement vectors $a_f,b_f$. The full marginal gives
$\sum_fc_fb_fb_f^T=I_5$. The actual original-coordinate maps are

$$
\begin{gathered}
B_f=\sqrt{c_f}\,b_f^TD^\dagger:\mathbb C^5\longrightarrow\mathbb C,
\qquad R_f=Da_f:\mathbb C\longrightarrow\mathbb C^5,\\
\operatorname{Enc}(X)=(B_fXB_f^\dagger)_f,\qquad
\operatorname{Dec}_f(y)=R_fyR_f^\dagger.
\end{gathered}
\tag{2.9}
$$

Here $\sum_fB_f^\dagger B_f=I_5$ and $R_f^\dagger R_f=1$.
Matrix sandwiches remain positive at every reference extension.
The encoder is TP, each decoder is TP on every matrix of its whole
scalar block, and each nonzero composite Kraus matrix $R_fB_f$ has
rank one. The flag is consumed in decoding. These formulas give the
same channel as the sum in (2.8), without continuous flags or access
to an averaging record.

The support bound $225$ follows from QRC's real-product Carathéodory
argument applied to this unchanged Choi matrix. That compression
concerns a realization of the whole channel; it need not preserve a
particular list of canonical generator representatives. $\square$

